---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Icon from '../../components/Icon.astro';
import Katex from '../../components/Katex.astro';
import '../../styles/paper.css';
import 'katex/dist/katex.min.css';

// Every number on this page is traceable to the paper's own text and is checked by
// `node scripts/verify-paper-claims.mjs --slug gtex` against
// ~/Documents/papers_summaries/gtex/extracted_text.txt (the PMC7737656 author manuscript).
const title = "The GTEx Consortium Atlas of Genetic Regulatory Effects Across Human Tissues";
const description = "Technical summary of the GTEx Consortium v8 flagship (Science 2020): cis- and trans-eQTL and sQTL discovery across 15,201 RNA-seq samples from 49 tissues of 838 donors, cell-type interaction QTLs, and integration with 87 GWAS.";
const readingTime = "26 min read";
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} type="article" noindex={true}>
  <article class="paper-article">
    <nav style="margin-bottom: var(--space-4);">
      <a href="/papers/" style="display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.35rem; font-size: var(--fs-small); color: var(--color-accent); font-weight: 600;">
        <Icon name="arrow-left" size={14} /> Back to Paper Summaries
      </a>
    </nav>

    <header class="paper-header">
      <div class="paper-meta-bar">
        <span class="paper-tag">Functional Genomics, eQTLs & Multi-Tissue Regulation</span>
        <span class="paper-venue-badge">Science · 11 September 2020 (Research Article)</span>
        <span class="paper-time-badge"><Icon name="clock" size={14} /> {readingTime}</span>
      </div>

      <h1 class="paper-title">{title}</h1>

      <div class="paper-authors-box">
        <div><strong>Authors:</strong> The GTEx Consortium. Lead analysts: François Aguet, Alvaro N. Barbeira, Rodrigo Bonazzola, Andrew Brown, Stephane E. Castel, Brian Jo, Silva Kasela, Sarah Kim-Hellmuth, Yanyu Liang, Meritxell Oliva, Princy Parsana … with Kristin G. Ardlie and Stephen B. Montgomery among the manuscript working group.</div>
        <div><strong>Affiliation:</strong> Broad Institute of MIT and Harvard; University of Chicago; Stanford University; New York Genome Center; and the wider GTEx Consortium</div>
        <div><strong>DOI:</strong> <a href="https://doi.org/10.1126/science.aaz1776" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" style="color: var(--color-accent);">10.1126/science.aaz1776</a> · Science 369(6509), 1318–1330</div>
      </div>
    </header>

    <p class="paper-lead">
      Most variants that genome-wide association studies implicate in human traits fall outside protein-coding
      sequence, which leaves the mechanism unstated: the variant is associated with the trait, but nothing says
      which gene it acts on, in which tissue, or how. The <strong>v8 release of the Genotype-Tissue Expression
      (GTEx) project</strong> is the reference answer to that question at scale. Analysing <strong>15,201 RNA-seq
      samples from 49 tissues of 838 post-mortem donors</strong>, GTEx v8 found <em>cis</em>-eQTLs for
      <strong>18,262 protein-coding genes — 94.7% of those detected</strong> — mapped <em>cis</em>-sQTLs for
      <strong>12,828 protein-coding genes (66.5%)</strong> from intron-excision ratios, recovered cell-type
      interaction QTLs hidden inside bulk tissue, and connected all of it to <strong>87 complex traits</strong>.
    </p>

    <div class="paper-toc">
      <div class="paper-toc-title">
        <Icon name="list" size={14} /> Table of Contents
      </div>
      <ol class="paper-toc-list">
        <li><a href="#summary">1. The v8 Dataset: What Was Actually Measured</a></li>
        <li><a href="#eqtl-discovery">2. cis- and trans-eQTL Discovery Across 49 Tissues</a></li>
        <li><a href="#splicing-sqtls">3. Splicing QTLs from Intron Excision Ratios (LeafCutter)</a></li>
        <li><a href="#fine-mapping">4. Fine-Mapping: From Association to Candidate Causal Variant</a></li>
        <li><a href="#cell-type-deconvolution">5. Cell-Type Specificity & Interaction QTLs (ieQTLs)</a></li>
        <li><a href="#gwas-colocalization">6. Integration with 87 GWAS: Enrichment and Mechanism</a></li>
        <li><a href="#mathematical-formulation">7. Mathematical Formulation: The QTL and Interaction Models</a></li>
        <li><a href="#failure-modes">8. Limitations, and Why They Matter Downstream</a></li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <section id="summary" class="paper-section">
      <h2>1. The v8 Dataset: What Was Actually Measured</h2>

      <h3>Scale, and the difference between collected and analysed</h3>
      <p>
        Two sample counts appear in this paper and they are not interchangeable. After quality control the v8
        data set consists of <strong>838 donors and 17,382 samples from 52 tissues and two cell lines</strong>.
        The QTL analyses use a subset: the <strong>49 tissues or cell lines with at least 70 individuals</strong>
        having both RNA-seq and whole-genome genotype data, comprising <strong>15,201 samples</strong>. The
        ≥70-individual floor is what makes the per-tissue association tests comparable, and it is why three
        tissues drop out of the analysis entirely.
      </p>

      <div class="paper-table-wrap">
        <table class="paper-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Dimension</th>
              <th>v8, as collected</th>
              <th>v8, as analysed for QTLs</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Donors</strong></td>
              <td>838 post-mortem donors</td>
              <td><strong>838 donors</strong></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>RNA-seq samples</strong></td>
              <td>17,382 samples</td>
              <td><strong>15,201 samples</strong></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Tissues</strong></td>
              <td>52 tissues + 2 cell lines (Fig. 1 illustrates 54 tissue types, including 11 brain regions)</td>
              <td><strong>49 tissues or cell lines</strong>, each with ≥70 individuals</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Genotypes</strong></td>
              <td colspan="2">Whole-genome sequencing on every donor</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h3>Who the donors are — and the constraint that follows</h3>
      <p>
        Of the 838 donors, <strong>715 (85.3%) were European American, 103 (12.3%) African American and 12
        (1.4%) Asian American</strong>. That distribution is the single most important caveat attached to every
        number below: allele frequencies, LD structure and therefore fine-mapping resolution are all estimated
        predominantly in one ancestry, and a causal variant common in another population may simply not be
        segregating here.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="eqtl-discovery" class="paper-section">
      <h2>2. cis- and trans-eQTL Discovery Across 49 Tissues</h2>

      <h3>The cis catalogue</h3>
      <p>
        Genes carrying an eQTL or sQTL are called <em>eGenes</em> and <em>sGenes</em>; the significant variants
        are <em>eVariants</em> and <em>sVariants</em>. Across all tissues, at 5% FDR per tissue, GTEx discovered
        <em>cis</em>-eQTLs for <strong>18,262 protein-coding and 5,006 lincRNA genes — 23,268 genes in total</strong>,
        corresponding to <strong>94.7% of all protein-coding and 67.3% of all detected lincRNA genes</strong>
        found in at least one tissue. The catalogue contains <strong>4,278,636 significant variants</strong>, which
        is <strong>43% of all variants with MAF ≥ 0.01</strong>.
      </p>

      <div class="paper-callout">
        <div class="paper-callout-title">
          <Icon name="sparkles" size={15} /> The saturation result, and what it means for bigger studies
        </div>
        <p>
          <em>cis</em>-QTL discovery tracks sample size closely — Spearman's ρ = <strong>0.95 for
          <em>cis</em>-eQTLs and 0.92 for <em>cis</em>-sQTLs</strong> against per-tissue sample size. But the
          gain from more samples is almost entirely in <em>small</em> effects: discovery of <em>cis</em>-eGenes
          with an over-two-fold effect <strong>saturates at roughly 1,500 genes in tissues with more than 200
          samples</strong>. Measured as allelic fold change, an average of <strong>22% of <em>cis</em>-eQTLs</strong>
          have an over-two-fold effect on expression. Sequencing more donors therefore buys statistical power,
          not new large-effect biology.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h3>The trans catalogue is two orders of magnitude smaller</h3>
      <p>
        Interchromosomal <em>trans</em>-eQTL mapping yielded <strong>143 <em>trans</em>-eGenes</strong> (121
        protein-coding and 22 lincRNA, at 5% FDR assessed per gene type) after controlling for false positives
        from read misalignment — the artefact that has historically inflated <em>trans</em> discovery. Effects
        are larger where they exist: over-two-fold effects on <em>trans</em>-eGene expression were observed for
        <strong>19% of <em>trans</em>-eQTLs</strong>. <em>trans</em>-sQTL mapping yielded <strong>29
        <em>trans</em>-sGenes</strong>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="splicing-sqtls" class="paper-section">
      <h2>3. Splicing QTLs from Intron Excision Ratios (LeafCutter)</h2>
      <p>
        Splicing QTLs were mapped in <em>cis</em> using <strong>intron excision ratios from LeafCutter</strong>,
        an annotation-free quantification that measures the relative usage of introns sharing a splice site
        rather than the abundance of annotated transcripts. This matters because it does not require the
        alternative isoform to be in the annotation to detect that its usage is under genetic control.
      </p>
      <p>
        GTEx discovered <strong>12,828 protein-coding (66.5%) and 1,600 lincRNA (21.5%) genes — 14,424 in
        total</strong> — with a <em>cis</em>-sQTL at 5% FDR per tissue in at least one tissue.
      </p>

      <div class="paper-table-wrap">
        <table class="paper-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Molecular phenotype</th>
              <th>Protein-coding genes</th>
              <th>lincRNA genes</th>
              <th>Total</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong><em>cis</em>-eQTL (expression)</strong></td>
              <td>18,262 (94.7%)</td>
              <td>5,006 (67.3%)</td>
              <td><strong>23,268</strong></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong><em>cis</em>-sQTL (splicing)</strong></td>
              <td>12,828 (66.5%)</td>
              <td>1,600 (21.5%)</td>
              <td><strong>14,424</strong></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong><em>trans</em>-eQTL</strong></td>
              <td>121</td>
              <td>22</td>
              <td><strong>143</strong></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong><em>trans</em>-sQTL</strong></td>
              <td colspan="2">—</td>
              <td><strong>29</strong></td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        The gap between the expression and splicing columns is the interesting part. Expression is under
        detectable genetic control for almost every protein-coding gene GTEx can see; splicing is under
        detectable genetic control for about two-thirds of them. Both are far commoner than the trans numbers,
        which is a statement about power as much as about biology.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="fine-mapping" class="paper-section">
      <h2>4. Fine-Mapping: From Association to Candidate Causal Variant</h2>
      <p>
        An eQTL is an association, and linkage disequilibrium means the associated variant is usually not the
        causal one. GTEx applied <strong>three independent fine-mapping methods — CaVEMaN, CAVIAR and
        dap-g</strong> — to infer likely causal variants for each <em>cis</em>-eQTL in each tissue.
      </p>

      <div class="paper-callout callout-info">
        <div class="paper-callout-title">
          <Icon name="target" size={15} /> How far fine-mapping actually gets you
        </div>
        <p>
          Using dap-g, the <strong>90% credible set contains a median of 6 variants</strong> per
          <em>cis</em>-eQTL — narrow enough to inspect, far too wide to call a mechanism. Only
          <strong>9.3% of <em>cis</em>-eQTLs have a variant with posterior probability above 0.8</strong>,
          which is the fraction where a single likely causal variant can be named. The other ~91% remain sets,
          not answers.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h3>A worked example: <em>CBX8</em></h3>
      <p>
        The paper's illustrative case is an eQTL for <em>CBX8</em> that colocalizes with breast cancer risk and
        with birth weight, at <strong>posterior probability 0.68 for both in lung</strong>. One of the three
        variants in its confident set overlaps and disrupts the binding motif of the transcription factor
        <strong>EGR1</strong>, and the role of EGR1 as the upstream driver is further supported by cross-tissue
        correlation. This is the shape of a complete mechanistic story — variant, motif, transcription factor,
        gene, trait — and the paper offers it as an example precisely because it is not the common case.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="cell-type-deconvolution" class="paper-section">
      <h2>5. Cell-Type Specificity & Interaction QTLs (ieQTLs)</h2>
      <p>
        A bulk tissue sample is a mixture. If a regulatory variant acts only in neutrophils, its effect in whole
        blood is diluted by every other cell type present, and the dilution varies from donor to donor with cell
        composition. GTEx turns that nuisance into signal: estimate each sample's cell-type fractions, then test
        whether a variant's effect size <em>scales with</em> the fraction of a given cell type.
      </p>

      <h3>The interaction QTLs recovered</h3>
      <p>
        The largest sets of cell-type interacting QTLs were <strong>1,120 neutrophil ieQTLs and 169 isQTLs in
        whole blood</strong>, and <strong>1,087 epithelial-cell ieQTLs and 117 isQTLs in transverse colon</strong>.
        Of these ieQTLs, <strong>76 and 229 respectively involved an eGene for which no QTL had been detected in
        the standard analysis</strong> — that is, the interaction model finds regulation that the bulk model
        cannot see at all, rather than merely re-describing what it already found.
      </p>

      <div class="paper-callout">
        <div class="paper-callout-title">
          <Icon name="sparkles" size={15} /> Why this is the most transferable idea in the paper
        </div>
        <p>
          Cell composition is a confounder in every bulk assay ever run. The ieQTL formulation says that when
          composition is <em>measured</em> rather than merely adjusted away, the composition-by-genotype
          interaction is itself a discoverable regulatory phenotype. The same construction reappears wherever a
          measurement averages over a heterogeneous population — which is also why single-cell data changed what
          questions could be asked of these tissues.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="gwas-colocalization" class="paper-section">
      <h2>6. Integration with 87 GWAS: Enrichment and Mechanism</h2>
      <p>
        For the trait analyses the consortium selected <strong>87 genome-wide association studies</strong>
        spanning binary and continuous complex traits with summary statistics in the public domain.
      </p>

      <h3>The enrichment, with the obvious confounder removed</h3>
      <p>
        Blood cellularity traits are a trap here: they are GWAS traits whose biology <em>is</em> cell
        composition, so they would inflate any enrichment computed in a tissue like whole blood. Repeating the
        enrichment analysis with those traits excluded gives <strong>5.21-fold enrichment for
        <em>trans</em>-eQTLs, 1.43-fold for <em>cis</em>-eQTLs and 1.81 for <em>cis</em>-sQTLs</strong>, largely
        preserving the pattern seen on the full trait set.
      </p>
      <p>
        The ordering is the finding. <em>trans</em>-eQTLs are far more enriched for trait associations than
        <em>cis</em>-eQTLs despite being ~160× rarer in the catalogue, which is what you would expect if
        <em>trans</em> effects sit closer to the trait in the causal chain — and it is also why the small
        <em>trans</em> catalogue is worth its cost.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="mathematical-formulation" class="paper-section">
      <h2>7. Mathematical Formulation: The QTL and Interaction Models</h2>

      <h3>The standard cis-QTL model</h3>
      <p>
        For gene <Katex tex="g" /> in tissue <Katex tex="t" />, expression is regressed on the genotype dosage
        of variant <Katex tex="v" /> with covariates for genotype principal components, PEER factors capturing
        hidden expression structure, sex and platform:
      </p>
      <div class="paper-equation-block">
        <Katex tex="Y_{g,t} = \beta_{v,g,t} X_v + \sum_{k=1}^{K} \gamma_k C_{k,t} + \epsilon_{g,t}" />
      </div>

      <h3>Effect size as allelic fold change</h3>
      <p>
        Reporting <Katex tex="\beta" /> from a model fitted to normalised expression makes effect sizes
        incomparable across genes. GTEx instead reports <strong>allelic fold change (aFC)</strong>, the ratio of
        expression attributable to the two haplotypes, which is why "an over-two-fold effect" is a meaningful
        threshold and why 22% of <em>cis</em>-eQTLs clearing it is a statement about biology rather than about
        units.
      </p>

      <h3>The interaction model</h3>
      <p>
        An ieQTL adds an explicit product term between genotype <Katex tex="X_v" /> and the estimated fraction
        <Katex tex="W_{\text{cell}}" /> of the cell type of interest. The interaction coefficient
        <Katex tex="\beta_{\text{int}}" /> — not the main effect — is the quantity tested:
      </p>
      <div class="paper-equation-block">
        <Katex tex="Y_{g,t} = \beta_0 X_v + \beta_{\text{int}} \left( X_v \cdot W_{\text{cell}} \right) + \alpha W_{\text{cell}} + \boldsymbol{\gamma}^{T} \mathbf{C} + \epsilon_{g,t}" />
      </div>
      <p>
        Both main effects must stay in the model. Dropping <Katex tex="\alpha W_{\text{cell}}" /> would let a
        pure composition effect — a gene simply more highly expressed in neutrophil-rich samples — masquerade as
        genotype-dependent.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="failure-modes" class="paper-section">
      <h2>8. Limitations, and Why They Matter Downstream</h2>

      <h3>What the tissues are</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Post-mortem, adult, steady-state.</strong> These are baseline adult tissues. Developmental stages, acute stimulation, and disease states are absent, so a variant that only acts under those conditions has no eQTL here to find.</li>
        <li><strong>Bulk, with composition estimated rather than observed.</strong> The ieQTL analysis mitigates this but depends on the quality of the deconvolution; a cell type without a clean marker signature is invisible to it.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>What the donors are</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>85.3% European American.</strong> Fine-mapping resolution, and therefore any downstream claim about a specific causal variant, inherits this ancestry composition along with its LD structure.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>What "we found an eQTL" does and does not license</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>A credible set of median size 6 is not a mechanism.</strong> Only 9.3% of <em>cis</em>-eQTLs resolve to a single variant at posterior probability &gt; 0.8. Colocalization between a GWAS signal and an eQTL is consistent with a shared causal variant; it does not establish one, and two distinct causal variants in LD produce the same picture.</li>
        <li><strong>Saturation is a warning to sequence-to-function modelling.</strong> Large-effect eGenes saturate by ~200 samples per tissue. A model trained to predict GTEx effect sizes is therefore learning mostly small effects estimated with wide intervals — which is a property of the label set, not of the model.</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="paper-callout callout-info">
        <div class="paper-callout-title">
          <Icon name="book" size={15} /> Source note
        </div>
        <p>
          This summary was written against the full text of the author manuscript deposited as PMC7737656
          (Science 369(6509), 1318–1330). Every figure quoted above appears in that text; the page is checked
          automatically by <code>scripts/verify-paper-claims.mjs</code>, which flags any number on it that the
          paper does not contain.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
